148
00:19:24.561 --> 00:19:38.572
<v Maya>But the tension we are left with is whether that equalization comes at the cost of automating away the necessary struggle of scientific training. The very friction that historically forged deep critical thinking is just disappearing.</v>

149
00:19:38.863 --> 00:19:53.852
<v Daniel>So for anyone listening who manages entry-level talent in a tech firm right now or, you know, for professors currently advising graduate students, how do you adapt to a landscape where the written word is no longer a reliable signal of competence?</v>

150
00:19:54.652 --> 00:20:00.116
<v Daniel>Gross and his colleagues ask an incredibly sobering question at the very end of their paper.</v>

151
00:20:00.516 --> 00:20:01.036
<v Maya>Yeah, they do.</v>

152
00:20:01.136 --> 00:20:15.622
<v Daniel>They ask, is the live in-person dissertation defense the only verification institution we have left? Which is wild to think about. We may be entering an era where we have to rely almost entirely on real time, unassisted human</v>

153
00:20:15.702 --> 00:20:20.645
<v Daniel>interrogation just to determine if someone actually possesses the expertise they claim to have on paper.</v>

154
00:20:21.335 --> 00:20:36.254
<v Maya>Which leaves us with a final thought to mull over. If AI completely removes the friction and the struggle of writing across all disciplines, not just in STEM, right, but in law, humanities, business, what other invisible, tacit skills are we</v>

155
00:20:36.434 --> 00:20:38.357
<v Maya>accidentally automating away?</v>

156
00:20:38.797 --> 00:20:53.176
<v Daniel>Yeah, what's getting lost? We are talking about the patience to just sit with a difficult problem, the structural logic required to build a long-form argument, and the self-awareness to spot your own flaws. Are we going to wake up a</v>

157
00:20:53.336 --> 00:21:00.304
<v Daniel>generation from now and realize we used our cognitive GPS so much that we completely forgot how to navigate the intellectual world?</v>
